<section xml:id="section-representations">
  <title>Group Actions and Representations</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this last section on group theory, I want to introduce one
      last definition that is central to group theory: groups actions.
      I don't want to get too deeply into the technique, but I want to
      use the idea of a group action to talk more about the
      classification of groups and the understanding of their
      structure. That will lead to representations, a major idea in
      groups theory. But first the main definition.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-actions">
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>S</m> be a set. An
          <term>action</term> of the group on the set is defined by
          the following properties. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              For all <m>g \in G</m> and for all <m>s \in S</m>, there
              is an operation (written multiplicatively by default)
              <m>gs</m> that produces a new element of the set. 
            </li>
            <li>
              If <m>g,h \in G</m> and <m>s \in S</m>, the the
              operation on the set satisfies this equation..
              <me>
                (gh)s = g(hs)
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              The identity elements in <m>G</m>, <m>e</m>, satisfies
              <me>
                es - s
              </me>
              for all <m>s \in S</m>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <ul>
            There is a function <m>f: G \times S \rightarrow S</m>
            with the same properties as the operaation defined above. 
          </ul>
          <ul>
            There is a homomorphism <m>:\phi G \rightarrow Sym(S)</m>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      All three versions of the definition capture the idea that the
      group is doing something to the elements of the set: permuting
      them, changing them, acting on them. We've already covered
      examples of groups that have this structure by definition: the
      dihedral groups and other geometric symmetry groups act on their
      geometric figures, the permutation groups act as symmetries. The
      definition here is generalizing these examples. Another groups
      which is not necessarily defined as functions on a set maybe be
      realized as functions on a set in this way. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Let me run through some examples to make this more clear. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        As I just mentioned, any groups that is explicitly defined as
        symmetries or permutations are already groups actions. The
        dihedral groups are group actions on the sets of vertices of
        polygons. The permutation groups <m>S_n</m> are group actions
        on <m>\{1, 2, \dots, n\}</m>. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Any groups explicitly defined as a group of functions is also
        already a group action, but only when the group operation is
        composition. The function groups under addition, like
        <m>C(\RR)</m>, are not naturally group actions on their
        domains. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Let <m>S</m> be the set of all circles in <m>\RR^2</m> (with
        any centre point). I can define an action of the group
        <m>\RR^2</m> on S: for any vector <m>(a,b) \in \RR^2</m> and
        any circle, I can shift that using the vector. This means the
        centre of the circle is moved <m>a</m> units in the <m>x</m>
        direction and <m>b</m> units in the <m>y</m> direction.
      </p>
      <p>
        To claim that this is a group action, I need to check the two
        properties. The first property says that if I do two different
        shifts, that is the same as adding the two vectors together
        and then shifting. This work: to shift by <m>a_1</m> in the
        <m>x</m> direction and then by <m>a_2</m> is equivalent to
        shifting ny <m>a_1 + a_2</m>. The second property says that
        the identity has not effect on the set. This also works: the
        identity is <m>(0,0)</m> and to shift by <m>(0,0)</m> is to
        leave the circle alone. This is a group action. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        The group <m>\RR^*</m> acts on <m>\RR^n</m> via the usual
        scalar multiplication from linear algebra. The identity in
        <m>\RR^*</m> is <m>1</m> and multiplication by <m>1</m>
        doesn't change any vector. Also scaling by <m>a</m> and then
        afterwards scaling by <m>b</m> is the same as scaling by
        <m>ab</m>. This satisfies both conditions, so it is a group
        action. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        The group <m>S_n</m> acts on <m>\RR^n</m> (or any other
        product, for that matter) by permuting the entries of the
        vectors. The identity leaves all the entries fixes, so it
        doesn't change any vector, and doing two permutations of the
        components is the same as first composition the permutations
        and then acting on the vector. This is an example of a general
        idea. We think of the permutation groups are permuting three
        <sq>symbols</sq>. However, many other mathematical
        constructions (not just symbols) can be permuted, like the
        ordered elements of a vector in this example. 
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-representations">
    <title>Representations</title>
    <p>
      The third equivalent definition of a group action is a
      homomorphism into a symmetric group of some set. This idea is
      also capture by the main definition of this section. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a group. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              A <term>representation</term> of the group is a
              homomorphism <m>\phi: G \rightarrow Sym(S)</m> into a
              symmetric group of some set <m>S</m>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              If the homomorphism is injective, this is called a
              <term>faithful representation</term>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              If <m>S = V</m> is a vector space, then the homomorphism
              is called a <term>linear representation</term> of the
              group. Usually, at least for finite vectors spaces, a
              linear representation is written <m>\phi: G \rightarrow
              GL(V,n)</m>. In this later case, this can also be called
              a <term>matrix representation</term>, since the elements
              of <m>GL(V,n)</m> are described by matrices.
            </li>
            <li>
              If <m>S</m> is a finite set, then the homomorphism is
              called a permutation representation (usually, the term
              permutation carries the connotation of re-arrangements
              of a finite set). Int his case, the homomorphisms is
              written <m>\phi: G \rightarrow S_n</m>. 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Representations are absolutely central in group theory. There is
      even a whole sub-discipline called Representation Theory. The
      idea is quite reasonable: permutations and linear algebra are
      very well understood pieces of mathematics, with many
      calculation techinques. If I discover a new group, its
      definition may arise in an area of mathematics with far fewer
      tools thatn permutations or linear algebra. It may be very hard
      to study the group in the situation where it arose. Therefore, I
      look for a representation, particularly a faithful
      representation, where I can study the group using the
      very well-developed tools of permutations or linear algebra. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore, we study groups by their representations. We find out
      about the structure of the group by understanding the structure
      of the representations. This was already hinted at when I talked
      about the Monster group and its 196,883 dimensional
      linear representation. Finding that representation means
      something about understanding that group (though actually
      calculating with those enormous matrices is, of course,
      unreasonable).   
    </p>
    <p>
      In the remainder of this section, I want to prove another
      structure theory, but now phrased as represenatation. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <title>The Regular Representation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a group. Then there is a group action of
          <m>G</m> on itself (considered as just a set). The action is
          defined almost tautologically as follows. Let <m>g, h \in
          G</m>, thinking of <m>g</m> as the group element acting and
          thinking of <m>h</m> as the set element being acted upon. 
          <me>
            gh \defeq gh 
          </me>
          This is obviously a bit silly. The notation of the left
          means the action of <m>g</m> on the set element <m>h</m>.
          The notation on the left means the product in the group
          using the normal binary operation. Both are typically
          written this way. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I need to check the two conditions. If <m>g_1, g_2 \in
          G</m>, then <m>(g_1g_2)h = <m>g_1(g_2h)</m> by the
          associativity of the group operation. But this is precisely
          the condition that I need for the group operation. The
          second condition is just <m>eh = h</m>, which is the
          definition of the identity. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      This might seem like a silly definition, but it has some notable
      value. It makes a really interesting observation: if we
      want, all groups are permutation groups. Just by acting on
      itself, every groups can be treated as a group of functions and
      the tools of functions and permutation can be brought to bare.
      This is useful. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This also give some explanation to the rows and columns of Caley
      tables. You may have noticed that each row and each columns has
      all of the group elements exactly once. You can now think of
      each row as a permutatin of the group, and it is precisely the
      permutatino given by multiplication by the element in that row. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
